  "query_preview": "..\nToday: A low sitting off the PNW coast is bringing overall \nsouthwest flow to the area, increasing PWATs to 100 to 150 percent \nof normal (0.75 to 1 inch). A few weak impulses moving through the \narea combined with the added moisture and daytime heading has led to \nthunderstorms in the Cascades, northern mountains central and \nnorthern ID Panhandle, and far SE Washington. Through the rest of \nSunday, these areas have a continued 10-20 percent chance of \nisolated thunderstorms. These thunderstorms have mainly been \nproducing lightning, gusty outflow winds 15-25 mph, and localized \nheavy downpours. Main impacts from these thunderstorms look to be \nany lightning induced fire starts. Other impacts being seen \nthroughout the forecast area include Wenatchee and Moses Lake, which \ncontinue to see degraded air quality due to being downwind of the \nLittle Giant Fire. Elsewhere, expect dry conditions and light winds. \n\nMonday through Thursday: By tomorrow afternoon, thunderstorm chances \nwill decrease as a drier air mass begins moving in. Potential \nthunderstorm coverage decreases dramatically, with only a 5 to 10 \npercent chance near far SE Washington and in the far northern \nCascades. With higher heights over the area, temperatures will \nincrease through the week. A dry trough will move through the area \non Tuesday, bringing wind gusts of 15-20 mph to the Cascade gaps and \nBasin areas during the afternoon and evening. Minimum relative \nhumidity values are forecast to drop to the high teens to low 20s. \nFor the lower Columbia Basin and Palouse areas, Hot Dry Windy Index \nvalues rise to above the 90th percentile. Tuesday will continue to \nbe monitored for any potential fire weather headlines. Winds \nwill relax by Wednesday and further on Thursday, decreasing fire\nweather concerns. Temperatures continue rising with the \nbuilding of the ridge, increasing back into the low to mid 90s \nby Thursday. \n\nFriday through Saturday: The low off the coast will finally begin \nmoving northeastward and across the state of Washington by Friday, \nand will usher in a robust dry cold front on Saturday. While Friday \nwill continue seeing temperatures in the low to mid 90s, Saturday \nlooks to see temperatures lower 4-6 degrees. Clusters initially were \nin disagreement about the evolution of the low, but now nearly 80 \npercent of ensemble members show the low approaching the WA/OR \nborder on Friday and a trough moving through on Saturday. Winds with \nthis frontal passage will be more widespread and reach 20 to 25 mph. \nHot, Dry, Windy Index values increase into the 70th percentile and \nabove for areas in the basin, Cascades, and Palouse. With these \nhigher wind gusts, Saturday also becomes a day to watch for potential\nfire weather concerns such as quick fire spread for any new and\nongoing fires. /AS",
